<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolesław IV the Curly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1122–<a href="/years/1173">1173</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Boleslaw IV the Curly · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolesław IV the Curly was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> and from 1146 High Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> after he and his younger brothers drove out their eldest brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-the-exile">Władysław II the Exile</a>. He had to submit to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> after the imperial invasion of 1157. On his death in 1173 his brother Mieszko III succeeded him as senior duke.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iv-the-curly.jpg" alt="Bolesław IV the Curly" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bonio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:O%C5%82bin-tympanon_fundacyjny-awers.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bolesław was the third son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> and his second wife Salomea of Berg. The deaths of his older brothers Leszek and Casimir by 1131 left him the eldest son of their parents. When his father died in 1138 he was 13, and under the testament he received the newly created Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, made up of Masovia and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>. In his first years he remained under the influence of his mother and the voivode Wszebor, who feared the ambition of his half-brother, High Duke Władysław II. Władysław tried to restore the unity of the country and deposed the junior dukes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Open conflict began in 1141, when Salomea met her sons at Łęczyca without Władysław's consent and betrothed her daughter Agnes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-ii-of-kiev">Mstislav II of Kiev</a>. Civil war broke out fully in 1145. The junior dukes gained the backing of the voivode Piotr Włostowic after Władysław had him blinded, and in 1146, after rebellions and an excommunication by the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, Władysław was defeated and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Bolesław took Silesia and the Seniorate Province at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, with the title of high duke. His brother Mieszko III kept Greater Poland and Henry received Sandomierz.</p><p>Władysław's wife Agnes persuaded her brother-in-law King Conrad III to intervene, but his expedition was defeated near the Oder in August 1146. In 1157 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> invaded and laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>. On 30 August 1157, at his camp in Krzyszkowo, Bolesław was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Empire. He paid a large tribute, swore loyalty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> on Christmas Day and gave his brother Casimir II as a hostage. Władysław was not restored and died in exile two years later.</p><p>Bolesław then proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the pagan Prussians, but efforts to conquer them were defeated in 1166, and his brother Henry of Sandomierz was killed. He added Sandomierz to the Seniorate Province against his father's testament, and in 1168 Casimir, Mieszko III and much of the Lesser Polish nobility rebelled. By dividing Henry's lands into three parts, Bolesław kept his rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bolesław gave many gifts to the Church, among them the Benedictine monastery at Trzemeszno, and around 1151 he founded the collegiate church at Czerwińsk. His eldest son, also Bolesław, died in 1172 aged sixteen. The same year Mieszko III rebelled again, and Barbarossa invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> a second time, ending the revolt with a peace made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. Bolesław died on 5 January 1173. His surviving son Leszek succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>, while Mieszko III succeeded him as high duke. Casimir II received Sandomierz and became high duke four years later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolesław IV the Curly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Krakow passed in turn to <b>Boleslaw IV</b>, Mieszko III and Casimir II the Just, and the seniority principle broke down as rival dukes fought for the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a></li><li><blockquote>Masovia, on the middle Vistula with its centre at Plock, was given to <b>Boleslaw IV the Curly</b> under the testament of Boleslaw III in 1138.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-masovia">Duchy of Masovia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1155" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Bolesław IV the Curly"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4450 5804h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4450 5804h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1155">What was here in 1155? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
